// The crowd solver doing its job in the configuration the game actually has,
// rather than in a cluster built to provoke it.
//
// It exists because of a surprise. `shoving_through_the_horde` walks due +X
// through the same 64 bodies and produces **zero** crowd contacts — its golden
// trace did not change by a single line when enemy-against-enemy separation was
// added. That looked like the new pass being inert, and it is not: the horde
// spawns on an axis-aligned grid 0.7 apart, and enemy contact distance is
// 2 * ENEMY_RADIUS = 0.5, so there is 0.2 of slack along each axis. Pushing
// *along* an axis slides bodies down the corridors between rows and never
// closes that slack.
//
// Push across the grid instead and they compress against their diagonal
// neighbours, where the slack is smaller relative to the direction of travel.
// Measured: 20 of the 60 ticks report crowd contacts, against 0 for the same
// shove along the axis.
//
// The lesson is about the *test*, not the solver: a horde laid out on a grid
// has an axis a scenario can travel along and see nothing. This is the
// companion that walks across it.
(
    description: "Shoved across its own grid the horde compresses, where shoved along it, it does not.",
    setup: (enemies: 64),
    inputs: [
        // Normalised on the way in, so this is a full-speed diagonal rather
        // than root-two times the speed.
        (at: 0, ticks: 60, dir: (1.0, -1.0)),
    ],
    budget: (ticks: 60),
    expect: (
        enemy_count: 64,
        trace: "shoving_diagonally_jostles_the_crowd.trace",
    ),
)
